/** @mainpage

This suite exercises tsf-sql - SQL database sessions from the Test
Agent it runs on - against PostgreSQL, SQLite and MariaDB. One test
matrix runs on all three: every test takes the engine as its
@c backend parameter.

- connection lifecycle and reconnection, CRUD, parameterised statements
  with every value type (binary included), transactions with savepoints
  and atomicity, normalised schema introspection;
- result comparison (ordered, unordered, NULL semantics, tolerances)
  and the database assertions, each also provoked to fail with its
  verdict;
- aggregates and grouping checked against values worked out by hand;
  a replica that follows its primary, whole transactions only, and
  refuses writes;
- concurrency: the read anomalies and the lost update at each isolation
  level, a deadlock, concurrent inserts and updates;
- driver errors, a lost connection, deadlines, cancellation and the
  release of resources after failures, a large result read in bounded
  batches;
- TLS and authentication failures, secrets kept out of messages, a
  read-only security audit with a known-bad control, privilege
  separation, SQL injection through an application input;
- performance measurements, and the equivalence of results between two
  engines.

There is no mock: the prologue starts a PostgreSQL and a MariaDB
container through the agent's docker client, with TLS and four accounts
each, and SQLite works on a temporary file. The database client is a
library linked into the agent's RPC server, driven in-process. A test
skips when its engine is not there. Every test emits an MI measurement
artifact with a line graph, so the HTML log and Bublik draw what was
measured.

*/
